    credit: "Executing Plans by Jesse Vincent (obra/superpowers) (MIT)",
    roles: ["coder"],
    triggers: ["execute the plan","follow the plan","next plan step","implement plan step","work through the plan"],
    purpose: "Carry out one plan step exactly, prove it with tests, and record any deviation as a ruling",
    instructions: "- Re-read this step's text before editing. What you remember is a summary; the step has the exact names and values.\n- Work the step's actions in order. Write and run the test before the code; a test that passes before the code exists is a finding about the test.\n- After each run_script, compare the output with what the step expects. Three outcomes: it matches (continue); the code is wrong (find the cause, do not patch the symptom); the plan is wrong (make the smallest change that satisfies the request).\n- Record every deviation in your task_complete summary as: Ruling: what you decided, why, what it costs if wrong. An unrecorded deviation is a hidden decision.\n- Decide ambiguities yourself. Stop with report_blocked only for: deleting data or files you did not create, security-sensitive changes, or a plan so broken every path is a guess.\n- Before task_complete: every test the step names exists and ran, the last test run passed, typecheck passed, and you read each output.\n- Keep long output small: read the tail of failures, not the full log.",
    acceptance: ["Every test named in the step exists and its last run passed.","Each deviation from the step is listed as a Ruling in the summary."],
