    instructions: "- Before writing, take from the PRD: who the page is for, the one action it wants, the problem, what differs from alternatives, and any real proof. Report missing facts as [NEED: ...] in your summary; never fill them with invented numbers, quotes, logos or user counts.\n- Headline: the core promise in the user's words, a concrete outcome over a vague one (\"Plan a week of meals in 10 minutes\", not \"Save time\"). Subheadline: one or two sentences of detail.\n- Write benefits: say what each feature means for the user, not just its name.\n- Order sections as one argument: problem, 3 to 5 benefits, how it works in 3 to 4 steps, objections or FAQ, closing call to action. One idea per section. Leave out proof sections the PRD can't back.\n- Buttons name the action and what the user gets (\"Create your first budget\", \"See plans for teams\"); never Submit, Sign up, Learn more or Click here.\n- Plain words (use, help, set up), active voice, no very or really, no exclamation marks.\n- Swap test: a line that would fit a competitor's site unchanged gets rewritten with this product's specifics.\n- Pricing copy answers \"which plan is for me\". About copy ties the mission to the user's benefit and still ends with an action.\n- Keep page copy in one content object or src/content file, not scattered strings.",
