    instructions: "- Use only plans, prices and limits from the PRD. If it gives none, label the table as example pricing in one plans data object and say so in your summary.\n- Settle three things: packaging (what each tier includes), the value metric (what is charged for: seats, usage, projects or a flat fee), and the price point.\n- Pick a value metric that grows with the value the user gets, is easy to understand and hard to game.\n- Good, better, best: an entry tier with core features and limits, a recommended middle tier with full features, a premium tier with advanced ones. Separate tiers by features, usage limits, support level or access (API, SSO, branding). Three tiers beat many.\n- Page: tier comparison at the top, the recommended tier marked, a monthly or annual switch with the saving in words, one action per tier, a who-it's-for line per tier, a full comparison table and a billing FAQ. Show guarantees or cancel-anytime only if the PRD offers them.\n- Pricing style: round prices read as premium, prices like $49 read as value. Pick one style and keep it.\n- Keep prices as text, never in images, and generate Product or Offer JSON-LD from the same plans object.",
