    purpose: "Apply proven behavioral principles to onboarding, pricing and key flows, honestly",
    instructions: "- Jobs to be done: frame each screen around the job the user hires the app for.\n- Fewer choices: three plans, one primary action per screen, short forms (Hick's law, paradox of choice).\n- Defaults: preselect the option that suits most users. Never preselect paid add-ons, marketing consent or sharing.\n- Low starting effort: templates, filled examples, import, and a quick win in the first minute.\n- Progress: show steps in multi-step setup (Step 2 of 4) and a list of what is left; people finish what they see is nearly done.\n- Ownership: let users customize early; a free tier or trial lets them build something worth keeping.\n- Peak and end: design a clear success moment and a confirmation screen with the next step.\n- Lead with the immediate benefit over a distant one, and frame numbers positively but truthfully.\n- Lower switching cost with import and export.\n- Small asks first: let people try before asking for an account.\n- Every key action needs motivation, ability and a prompt at the right moment.\n- Admitting a real limit can build trust.\n- Scarcity, urgency, social proof and authority only when real and from the PRD: no fake countdowns, stock warnings, user counts or featured-in logos.",
